In article <1994May24.191203.28855@martha.utcc.utk.edu> dbd@martha.utcc.utk.edu (David DeLaney) writes:
>Jeremy Matthew Toeman <Shaggy+@CMU.EDU> writes:
>>What are the easiest ways to identify the following:
>>1] Rings
>
>Wear them, or drop them down a sink.
>
	It's a _very_ good idea to find out if they're cursed first, either
by pet or alter.  The usual thing is to drop the cursed ones down a sink,
and put on the noncursed--which frequently won't tell you anything, so save
them for a scroll of identify.
	Also, if you kill a Nazgul it's always carrying a ring of invisibility,
which isn't exactly what they had in LOR, but it's as close as nethack gets ;-)

>>3] Potions
>
>Drink them. Or dip a unicorn horn in them.
>
	Or throw them at an adjacent wall.  They'll smash, and the vapors can
frequently tell you what they do.

>> How can you tell if food is tainted?

>If your pet won't eat it; if it's been more than a few turns since you killed
>it.

	"If your pet won't eat it" doesn't do you much good if _you_ wanted
to eat it! ;-)  Note that (I'm pretty sure) larger corpses spoil faster
than smaller ones, and zombie corpses are never any good.
>
>>What should you not eat (like humans and pets), and why not?
>
>Generally, you shouldn't eat your own race or a pet because your god'll
>get mad at you; there's several things that are poisonous, but if you have
>resist poison you'll be fine. Tainted food will kill you quick, so you'll
>have to #pray...

	Or use a (blessed?) potion of extra healing, or cast cure sickness,
or use a unicorn horn.

